/**
 * Crow Studio — the panel's browser client, as one inline script.
 *
 * The dashboard panel emits its content into an existing page, so the client
 * travels as a string (`CLIENT_SCRIPT`) and is written as an EXPRESSIONS ONLY
 * classic script: it contains no backtick and no `${`, which is the rule every
 * panel in this repo keeps so the script can never be mistaken for a template.
 * For the same reason the emitted text uses single-quoted strings only — no
 * double quote and no backslash anywhere — and builds the DOM with
 * `createElement` + `textContent`, never with HTML strings. Song titles,
 * track names and every other piece of document data therefore cannot inject
 * markup.
 *
 * Two pages share the script:
 *
 *   - the song library (`data-studio-library`): the song list read from
 *     `GET /api/studio/songs`, and the "New song" form;
 *   - one song (`data-studio-song="<id>"`): the session grid, the clip editor,
 *     the transport, the history and the presence row.
 *
 * ## The shell and the transport bar
 *
 * The song page is a page of widgets around one top bar and one transport bar,
 * and the handler inlines those two scripts (and the layout model they read)
 * BEFORE this one: `panel/shell.js` moves the handler's own nodes into the top
 * bar and into widget frames, and `panel/transport-bar.js` takes the transport
 * controls into its bar. Nothing here is cloned, so this script keeps finding
 * the same elements. What this script owes them is at boot and at the end of
 * every render: the operator's default layout template is fetched once (with a
 * 3 s cap) and handed to the song through `StudioShellLayout.seedSong` before
 * the shell mounts, both controllers are mounted and refreshed, and both are
 * destroyed by `teardown`. Where a widget sits, which tab is open and which
 * mode the page is in are VIEW STATE: they live in this browser (or, for the
 * operator's template, in an instance setting), never in the song document and
 * never in the undo history.
 *
 * The song page is a read replica of the hub's Yjs document over
 * `/api/studio/ws` (see server/web.js for the frames): `state` carries the
 * full document and the last 50 history steps, `update` carries one applied
 * command with its entry, and every edit the page makes goes back as a
 * `command`/`undo`/`redo` frame with a client-generated `cmd_id` so a resend
 * after a reconnect applies once. Yjs itself is loaded on demand from
 * `/dashboard/studio/assets/yjs.mjs`.
 *
 * Playback is local: the first Play click creates the AudioContext (browsers
 * only start audio from a gesture) and builds the engine from
 * `/dashboard/studio/assets/engine-client.js`. Nothing about audio is sent to
 * the hub.
 *
 * ## Recording takes
 *
 * An audio track's header arms it, picks its input (raw: echo cancellation,
 * noise suppression and auto gain off), shows the interface's measured round
 * trip and can send the input through Studio's own output so the operator
 * hears how late it is.
 *
 * Record, with a track armed, arms a second worklet on the SAME context as the
 * engine, so the recorder and the sequencer share one clock: the take starts on
 * the exact frame a launch (or the next bar) reported, and ends on the frame the
 * loop pass ends on. While the clip loops, every pass is split into its own take
 * (`recorder.splitAt`), and the interface's calibrated round trip
 * (`offset_frames`) both keeps the tail of the last pass and is recorded on the
 * take. A take armed now is cut by the engine's whole master delay as well — the
 * limiter's look-ahead and the mix delay a converted amp's compensation adds, as
 * the page tracks it in `view.mix.latency` and reads it where the take starts —
 * so it lands where it was played; a take recorded before that is never moved.
 *
 * The take is local first: every chunk is written to IndexedDB
 * (`panel/take-store.js`) before anything is sent, the upload begins when the
 * network allows, and a take this device still owes is resumed on the next
 * load. A finish that returns the take is the only thing that lets the chunks
 * go. The row above the editor says where the take is: `Saved on this device —
 * will upload.` or `Uploading 3 of 12`.
 *
 * Nothing that can still be saved is dropped. A pass uploads as soon as its last
 * chunk is here, a chunk this device cannot keep stops the recording and
 * uploads what was kept, a page that is left mid-take stops the recorder and
 * waits for its last chunk, and a recording the tab never finished is turned
 * back into a take on the next load. One uploader per take (Web Locks), one
 * count of the chunks the gateway has confirmed, and a refusal the gateway will
 * repeat is shown on the row with its own Try again and Remove buttons.
 *
 * ## The takes lane
 *
 * An audio clip's editor is its takes lane instead of the piano roll: the comp
 * bar (each segment labelled with its take's number and coloured from it), one
 * row per take newest first, and this device's own copy of the audio.
 *
 * The rows are the plan's: `Take <n>`, its length in seconds, who recorded it,
 * an "uncompensated" badge when the take carries no measured round trip, a play
 * button, and `Keep`, `Discard` or (for a discarded take, which is dimmed and
 * listed after the live ones) `Restore`. Keep, Discard and Restore are one
 * `take.*` command each, so each is one step in the shared history.
 *
 * A comp is chosen by dragging on the comp bar while a take row is selected:
 * the range snaps to one beat (a quarter of one with Shift held), replaces what
 * it covers, merges with adjacent pieces of the same take, stays inside the
 * clip, and goes to the hub as ONE `clip.set_comp` for the whole new segment
 * list when the pointer comes up. The play button auditions one take with a comp
 * this page makes up and hands to the engine — nothing is sent, so an audition
 * never becomes a history step. `Remove from this device` clears the take
 * store's audio cache (spec §12) after a yes, and touches no take this device
 * has not uploaded yet.
 *
 * A take recorded under a punch range writes the comp itself, ONCE, when the
 * recording is over: the passes it landed folded into the comp in ascending pass
 * order, each over its own range, so the latest pass owns the beats two ranges
 * share. That write and a comp drag's are queued per clip, so a drag always
 * splices onto the comp the punch's write left.
 *
 * ## The timeline
 *
 * The header's Session / Arrangement switch chooses what the transport plays
 * and what the page shows: the scene grid, or the song laid out on bars. The
 * choice is kept per song in `localStorage` when the browser keeps storage, and
 * the switch works without it. Switching tells the engine (`setMode`), which is
 * what makes the transport play the arrangement instead of the grid.
 *
 * The timeline is the approved design (mockups artboard 5): a ruler with a
 * section row, the markers and the bar numbers, one lane per track, and the
 * song's placements as blocks — in the track's colour, outlined in the colour
 * of whoever placed them (the operator amber, an agent blue), white when
 * selected, with a waveform outline and fade corners on an audio lane. A white
 * playhead follows the engine's own position, and the ruler seeks: a click on a
 * whole bar, and a drag at beat resolution, whether the transport is running or
 * paused. Pause stops it where it stands and the next Play carries on from
 * there. Every command that takes a bar takes the bar line at or before the
 * playhead -- the line the cut mark draws and the Split button names -- and a
 * fractional playhead is never handed to one.
 *
 * Every edit is ONE command per pointer release, snapped to whole bars (a fade
 * to a quarter of a beat): a clip dragged out of the session grid is placed on
 * the bar it was let go over (the lanes come up under it, and the clip editor's
 * `Place on timeline` is the keyboard path — bar 1 from the session view, which
 * has no playhead, the playhead's bar from the timeline), a placement drags to
 * move, its right edge resizes, its corners fade, and Delete or an arrow key
 * removes or moves the selected one. A pointer the browser cancels ends its
 * drag without writing anything, and a render puts the keyboard back on the
 * block that had it. Clicking the marker row adds a marker, dragging the
 * section row adds a section, and both are renamed and removed from their own
 * focusable buttons.
 *
 * `Record to timeline` collects the launches of a performance played in the
 * session view — a launch opens a placement on its bar, the next launch on that
 * track (or the stop bar) closes it — and any stop of the transport (the Stop
 * button, the end of a take) writes them all as one
 * `arrangement.record_performance`, so one Undo takes the whole performance
 * back. A refusal keeps the performance on the page, with the hub's sentence
 * and the two ways out of it: place the part that fits, or discard it.
 * Recording with the transport in the arrangement view records into the
 * placement under the playhead on the armed track, making the clip in the first
 * scene whose slot on that track is empty and the placement where the playhead
 * is; with no empty slot it says so and arms nothing. An audio lane's `Takes`
 * button opens the clip editor's own take rows under the lane, comp bar and
 * all.
 *
 * ## Sounds into a pad
 *
 * The pad-and-steps view puts a sound on a pad in three ways, and all three end
 * in the same place: a sample upload staged in this device's take store and
 * sent through the take routes (`kind: "sample"`).
 *
 * `Import a sound` decodes the file once (`decodeAudioData` is allowed for an
 * import only), re-encodes it as 24-bit PCM and stages it. `Record into pad`
 * arms a recorder on this very frame, with no bar wait and no latency
 * compensation. `Resample output` taps the master mix or one track's own output;
 * with Snap to bar on while the transport runs it keeps exactly the frames from
 * the next bar line to the bar line after the second press, and with the
 * transport stopped it keeps the frames from the first block it is handed to the
 * block the second press lands in. Every tap block names the frame it starts on,
 * so those cuts are on the frame itself, never on a count of blocks.
 *
 * Every path keeps the recorder's own rule: PCM is cut into chunks of
 * floor(1 MiB / a frame's bytes) WHOLE FRAMES, so the gateway can join the staged
 * chunks as one byte stream. A resample's blocks and a pad recording's chunks
 * are written to the take store as they fill, so a reload recovers them; the
 * pending row carries no chunk count until every chunk is stored, exactly as a
 * take's does. One recording at a time: a take, a pad recording and a resample
 * never run together, a second press while a pad recording is still being set up
 * cancels it (no recorder is left orphaned), and the input a pad recording
 * opened is closed when it ends unless it is the armed track's own. A sample is
 * capped at 60 s, and a recording stops by itself there. A chunk this device
 * cannot keep stops it and uploads what was kept, as a take's does.
 *
 * ## Chopping a sample
 *
 * The chop editor draws the selected pad's sample: its name, how many slices it
 * has, which one the pad plays, a waveform built from one peak per pixel column
 * of the decoded PCM, and one marker per slice start. `Auto-chop by hits` (with
 * its own sensitivity) and `Grid` (2, 4, 8 or 16) run `engine/chop.js` — in a
 * Worker when the browser has one, inline from the same file when it does not —
 * and the result is ONE `slice.set` of the whole slice set, with every slice
 * whose start has not moved keeping its id. A marker is dragged: it follows the
 * pointer and moves its slice's start and the previous slice's end, and the
 * single `slice.set` goes out on the pointer's release, never during the drag.
 * A `slice.set` the hub refuses (a slice a pad still plays cannot go) leaves the
 * document alone: its sentence shows on the editor and the waveform is drawn
 * again from the snapshot. `Assign slices to pads` fills the pads from the
 * selected one on, one `pad.assign` each, and names in one notice any pad it
 * skipped because it already holds another sound.
 *
 * ## The steps, the patterns and the chain
 *
 * A pattern clip's editor is the step sequencer: the clip's patterns as tabs
 * (`+ Pattern`, rename on a double-click, remove), the clip's own loop (its
 * length in step positions, its step length and its swing — they take the clip
 * and every pattern of it follows), and the selected pad's lane in the pattern
 * being shown. Sixteen step buttons to a page, four pages, and the page follows
 * the playhead while the transport runs. A click toggles one step; an on step's
 * velocity is the height of its fill. A step held down turns the pad controls
 * into locks: the control's release sends one `step.set` carrying that one lock
 * (the pad itself is untouched), the step wears a dot, and `Clear locks` on a
 * held or selected step sends one `step.clear_locks`. The chain is a row of
 * chips in the order the clip plays them: add the shown pattern to the end,
 * move a chip, or take one out, each change ONE `chain.set` with the whole new
 * chain.
 *
 * Everything here is drawn from the document snapshot, so an agent's edit to a
 * step, a slice or the chain appears without a reload.
 *
 * ## Motion
 *
 * The pad tools' **Motion** arm records a curve: from the moment a numeric pad
 * control of the selected pad moves while the transport plays this pattern clip,
 * every move — from the controls here, or from a MIDI `pad.param` or macro sweep —
 * is a point on that field's lane, at the quarter-step the transport stands on
 * (a later value at one position replaces the earlier one). The control still
 * sounds and still writes its own `pad.set`, exactly as it does unarmed; motion
 * is recorded beside it. A pad control moved in LOCK MODE is a step lock and
 * nothing else: a lock is the value that voice keeps, so it records no motion.
 *
 * A take belongs to the pattern the transport is actually PLAYING on the clip's
 * track — the chain slot it stands in — not to the pattern the editor happens to
 * show, so a curve lands on the pattern it was played over. One pass of that
 * pattern after the first value — or a disarm, a Stop, another pattern — ends the
 * take, and each touched lane goes to the hub as ONE `motion.write` merged with
 * the points the curve already holds outside the span that was just played (a
 * merge that would pass the document's 4096 points is thinned, keeping the span
 * just recorded, so the write is never refused). An idle MIDI flush never starts
 * a new take: the move that made the point already recorded it. Leaving the page
 * mid-take writes what was recorded, like the knob's last value. `motion.clear`
 * is the lane's Clear button. A pattern's motion is drawn under its steps as one
 * line per lane of the selected pad, with the curve as one bar per point.
 *
 * ## Ask the agent
 *
 * The song page's ask box talks to the song project's bot: on load the client
 * asks `GET /api/studio/songs/<id>/project-bot` for it, and with a bot the
 * message goes to the bot's Perch interactive session: one
 * `POST /dashboard/perch-api/bots/<id>/interactive` carrying `briefing: true`,
 * then `POST /dashboard/perch-api/interactive/<sid>/message` per question, with
 * the bot's replies read from that session's SSE stream (`text`/`reply`
 * frames) and drawn under the box.
 *
 * The person's own message is drawn the moment they press Send, before any
 * network call, with a working line under it (`<name> is working…`) that stays
 * until the turn's `reply` frame lands. A small counter holds that line up: it
 * goes up per accepted send and down per `reply` frame, and a failed post takes
 * it down too. A turn that sends no frame at all for 180 s is reported instead
 * (`<name> has not answered yet…`) rather than spinning forever.
 *
 * A reply is drawn as the SERVER rendered it: a `text`/`reply` frame carries
 * `html` beside its plain `text` (`renderBotMarkdown` — marked plus
 * sanitize-html's allow-list, the same function Perch uses), and only that one
 * field is ever assigned to `innerHTML`. A frame without `html` — a failed
 * render, an older gateway — is drawn as text nodes, never as markup. Inside a
 * reply, a link to one of THIS song's renders becomes a player card (audio,
 * a `Download` link, and the measured loudness table the render's info route
 * answers with), built from the song id and the validated name alone; any other
 * storage address — a presigned one, or another song's render — is replaced by
 * `(link removed)`, so a signed URL is never kept, followed or shown.
 *
 * Two answers leave the box disabled with a note that says why: no bot at all
 * (`Set up a bot`, a link to Bot Builder's new-bot page for the project), and a
 * bot whose def grants no Studio tools (`Give <name> Studio tools`, one press on
 * `POST .../project-bot/studio-tools`; the page itself posts nothing). The
 * granted bot's session is forgotten, because one opened before the grant has
 * no Studio tools.
 *
 * The spawn's `briefing` field is honoured ONLY as the boolean `true`: the
 * gateway then rebuilds the project briefing from its own database and never
 * reads a word of client text. Studio's own context — the song, the tools and
 * the rules — therefore travels in front of the FIRST message a session
 * receives, and later messages are sent exactly as typed. One session per song
 * is kept in `sessionStorage` under `crow-studio-ask:<song id>` and reused for
 * as long as it keeps answering 2xx, so coming back to the song carries on in
 * the same conversation; teardown never stops it.
 *
 * ## Instruments
 *
 * A soundfont track plays one preset of one bank of the song's library, and its
 * header is where that is chosen. `+ Instrument` adds such a track, pointed at
 * the song's first font when it has one and at nothing when it has none (then
 * the header offers `Get the default instruments`).
 *
 * The presets are the BANK's, so only the engine can read them: the page fetches
 * the font's file route once per library id per page, hands it to
 * `loadLibrary`, and lists the `presets` the processor answers with as a
 * `bank:program Name` list box (bank 128 is `Drums`). A bank the engine cannot
 * parse yet is reported where it is used: `Loads when you stop.` while the
 * transport runs, and the reason itself when the parse failed ("This bank has
 * no instruments."). A file route that answers 404 is a bank this device does
 * not hold: the track says `Get this instrument` for a catalog entry, or `This
 * bank is not on this device.` for an upload.
 *
 * Every pick is ONE command — `track.set_program {track_id, bank, program}` for
 * a preset, `track.set_font {track_id, font_id}` for a bank — and a pick the
 * agent made wears the same outline as the rest of an agent edit. A CC-BY
 * bank's credit sits under the picker.
 *
 * `Add your own bank…` takes an SF2 or SF3 file: its first twelve bytes are read
 * HERE, in the browser, and a file that is not `RIFF….sfbk` is refused before a
 * single request (`That file is not an SF2 or SF3 bank.`), as is one past 512 MB.
 * The rest goes through the take routes in 1 MiB chunks (`kind: "font"`), and
 * finish answers with the library entry the song now holds. The note above the
 * input says who is responsible for that bank's licence.
 *
 * ## The mixer
 *
 * The Mixer view (plan 1d, Task 10) is the plan's third mockup drawn in this
 * panel's own look. It draws one strip per track, then the returns, then the
 * master, inside a box that scrolls sideways on its own, so a phone scrolls the
 * strips and never the page.
 *
 * A strip holds its effect chain as chips — one per insert, added from a menu
 * built from `EFFECT_PARAMS`, dragged to reorder, each with its own bypass
 * switch and remove — its level, its pan, its mute and solo and, on a track,
 * one row per send (level and pre/post) plus a way to add one. A chip opens
 * that insert's editor, whose one control per parameter comes from the same
 * table; a parameter's change is ONE `insert.set` per gesture (a drag is
 * debounced and its own release closes the window). The master strip carries
 * its chain and its gain, the four loudness readings the engine meters, the
 * song's target and the master's whole delay as its own small line (the
 * limiter's look-ahead plus any amp compensation).
 *
 * An agent's mix pass — the newest run of consecutive mixer steps by one agent
 * — is outlined in the agent's colour, with A/B and Undo pass above the strips.
 * **A/B** builds the song as it was BEFORE the pass by writing each step's
 * recorded before-values (mixer refs only) over a LOCAL copy of the current
 * snapshot and handing that copy to the engine: it never sends a command, and
 * any document change leaves it, back on the live song. **Undo pass** takes the
 * steps back newest first, one undo command at a time, waits for each answer
 * and stops at the first refusal with the sentence the plan fixed; undoing a
 * single step from its history row is still the row's own Undo, and the rest of
 * the pass stays outlined.
 *
 * An insert the engine had to bypass (it produced a sample that was not a
 * number) shows the engine's own sentence on its chip, and one whose effect is
 * still loading says `Loading…`. Neither is a document change.
 *
 * A SECOND amp on a chain that already holds one is a proposal: the agent made
 * it, and it plays nothing until the operator says yes. Its chip is dimmed and
 * names the sounds it would play (`Proposed: <capture> - <cabinet>`, the cabinet
 * only when it has one), and instead of a bypass switch the row offers **Accept**
 * (the people-only `insert.accept`, sent with the chain, capture and cabinet the
 * row was DRAWN with, so a proposal that moved since the draw is refused rather
 * than accepted unseen) and **Decline** (the ordinary `insert.remove`).
 *
 * ## The amp (plan 1d, Task 15)
 *
 * "Amp" is the last row of the "+ effect" menu. An amp is not an effect with
 * settings: it plays a NAM capture through a cabinet IR, so picking it opens its
 * own editor instead of adding an insert at once — an amp exists from the moment
 * it has a capture, and that pick IS the `insert.add`. The finished editor shows
 * the capture picker (the song's captures and the starter sounds this bundle
 * ships, with the credit line of the chosen one), the cabinet picker (No cabinet,
 * the song's cabinets and the starter cabinets), and the three numbers from
 * `AMP_PARAMS` (input, output, IR mix). A capture or a cabinet the operator
 * chose carries the line "Only you can change this amp's capture.", because that
 * file is a library entry and only the person at the page adds or changes one.
 * A starter sound is added through the gateway's own people-only route first, so
 * picking one is still ONE command on the document.
 *
 * An armed track's monitor switch and its measured delay are 1b's: it reads
 * "Hear the chain while recording" now that the input really runs through the
 * chain (Task 13), and while it is on the chain's own measured delay is its own
 * line — "Adds 3 ms" — beside the round-trip sentence.
 *
 * ## Turbo Drive
 *
 * The dashboard runs Turbo, so this page is snapshotted for the Back button and
 * the script can run again on the restored body. One session owns one page
 * element (`page.__studioSession`): a second boot while that session is alive
 * does nothing, and teardown — on `turbo:before-cache`, and on
 * `turbo:before-visit` so a visit that never starts cannot leave a live socket
 * behind — closes the socket and engine, removes the page's listeners, drops
 * `data-studio-ready` and empties the live regions. What Turbo caches is
 * therefore a static shell, and the next boot rebuilds it from the hub.
 */

/**
 * Every command id the panel can send — the GUI half of the spec's parity
 * rule, and the source of the `var COMMANDS = [...]` line in the script. A
 * registry id missing here, or an id here the registry does not know, is a bug
 * (tests/studio-parity.test.js checks both directions).
 *
 * The take commands follow the 1a ones. `take.add` is not here because a take
 * is never something the GUI sends: the gateway's upload finish route adds it
 * as Studio's own step once the WAV is in storage.
 *
 * The timeline commands close the list: `arrangement.*` lays clips out on the
 * bars, and `marker.*` and `section.*` name bars and ranges of them.
 *
 * The sampler commands follow: a kit's `bank.*` and `pad.*`, the song's
 * `sample.*` and `slice.*`, a pattern clip's `pattern.*`, `step.*` and
 * `chain.*`, and `clip.add_pattern` that makes one. `motion.write` and
 * `motion.clear` are the pattern's motion lanes: one lane per pad field, each
 * its own undo step. `pad.clear` is here because the panel's **Clear pad** is
 * the only way to run it: it is people-only, so it has no MCP tool.
 * `sample.add` is not here for the same reason `take.add` is
 * not: only the gateway's sample upload finish route adds a sample, as Studio's
 * own step.
 *
 * The MIDI commands end the list: `midi.map` routes one message, `midi.accept`
 * is the operator's yes to a proposal (`midi.accept` is here for the same reason
 * `pad.clear` is — it is people-only, and the panel's **Accept** button is the
 * only way to run it), `midi.remove` takes a row away, and
 * `midi.propose_profile` fills a device's rows in one step.
 *
 * The instrument commands close it (plan 1d): `track.set_font` and
 * `track.set_program` are what the instrument picker sends, one of them per
 * pick, and `library.remove` is the panel's own way to tidy an entry no track
 * plays. `library.add` is not here for the reason `sample.add` is not: only the
 * gateway's library routes add a file, as Studio's own step.
 *
 * The mixer commands follow (plan 1d, Task 6): `insert.add`, `insert.remove`,
 * `insert.move`, `insert.set` and `insert.bypass` are one step each in the
 * effect chain, `return.add`, `return.set` and `return.remove` are the returns,
 * `send.set` is one track's send to one return, and `master.set` is the master
 * strip. Each is one field write of one object, which is what makes a control
 * move one undoable step (Task 10). `insert.accept` is here beside them for the
 * reason `midi.accept` is: it is people-only, and the **Accept** button on a
 * proposed second amp is the only way to run it — the row sends the chain,
 * capture and cabinet it was drawn with.
 * @type {readonly string[]}
 */
export const COMMANDS = Object.freeze([
  "song.set_meta",
  "track.add",
  "track.remove",
  "track.rename",
  "track.set_device",
  "track.set_param",
  "track.set_mix",
  "track.set_font",
  "track.set_program",
  "track.lock",
  "track.unlock",
  "scene.add",
  "scene.rename",
  "scene.remove",
  "clip.write",
  "clip.edit_notes",
  "clip.set_length",
  "clip.rename",
  "clip.remove",
  "take.discard",
  "take.restore",
  "take.keep",
  "take.set_offset",
  "clip.set_comp",
  "clip.add_audio",
  "arrangement.place",
  "arrangement.move",
  "arrangement.resize",
  "arrangement.split",
  "arrangement.set_fades",
  "arrangement.remove",
  "arrangement.record_performance",
  "marker.add",
  "marker.rename",
  "marker.remove",
  "section.add",
  "section.set",
  "section.remove",
  "bank.add",
  "bank.rename",
  "pad.set",
  "pad.set_bank",
  "pad.assign",
  "pad.clear",
  "sample.rename",
  "slice.set",
  "pattern.add",
  "pattern.rename",
  "pattern.remove",
  "pattern.set_length",
  "pattern.set_steps_beat",
  "pattern.set_swing",
  "step.toggle",
  "step.set",
  "step.clear_locks",
  "motion.write",
  "motion.clear",
  "chain.set",
  "clip.add_pattern",
  "clip.copy_to_scene",
  "midi.map",
  "midi.accept",
  "midi.remove",
  "midi.propose_profile",
  "library.remove",
  "insert.add",
  "insert.accept",
  "insert.remove",
  "insert.move",
  "insert.set",
  "insert.bypass",
  "return.add",
  "return.set",
  "return.remove",
  "send.set",
  "master.set",
]);

/** The `var COMMANDS = [...]` declaration, built from the one list above. */
const COMMANDS_LINE =
  "  var COMMANDS = [" + COMMANDS.map((id) => "'" + id + "'").join(", ") + "];";

/**
 * The timeline's zoom arithmetic, as the exact lines the page runs.
 *
 * Zoom is VISUAL: it changes how wide the lanes (and the ruler over them) are
 * drawn, so the timeline scrolls sideways in its own container. The song
 * document and the engine never see it. A bar's x is its distance from the
 * first bar times the base spacing times the zoom factor, which is what makes
 * one step double (or halve) every lane and the ruler with it.
 * @type {readonly string[]}
 */
const ZOOM_LINES = Object.freeze([
  "  // How many pixels one bar takes at zoom 1, before the zoom factor.",
  "  var TIMELINE_BASE_PX = 32;",
  "",
  "  // The pixel x of a bar at one zoom, measured from the first bar line.",
  "  function zoomedBarX(bar, zoom, basePx) {",
  "    var at = Number(bar);",
  "    var scale = Number(zoom);",
  "    var base = Number(basePx);",
  "    if (!Number.isFinite(at)) at = 0;",
  "    if (!(scale > 0)) scale = 1;",
  "    if (!(base > 0)) base = TIMELINE_BASE_PX;",
  "    return at * base * scale;",
  "  }",
]);

/** The zoom arithmetic as one script fragment, for the named exports below. */
const ZOOM_SCRIPT = ZOOM_LINES.join("\n");

/**
 * The zoom arithmetic, evaluated from the very lines the page inlines. The
 * wrapper is a function of its own, so the page's own `var`s never land on
 * this module's scope.
 */
const zoomApi = new Function(
  "return (function () {\n" + ZOOM_SCRIPT + "\n  return { base: TIMELINE_BASE_PX, zoomedBarX: zoomedBarX };\n})();",
)();

/** How many pixels one bar takes at zoom 1. @type {number} */
export const TIMELINE_BASE_PX = zoomApi.base;

/**
 * The pixel x of a bar on the zoomed timeline: its distance from the first bar
 * line times the base spacing times the zoom factor.
 * @param {number} bar - the bar index (0 is the first bar)
 * @param {number} zoom - the zoom factor, one of the timeline's five steps
 * @param {number} basePx - the pixels one bar takes at zoom 1
 * @returns {number}
 */
export function zoomedBarX(bar, zoom, basePx) {
  return zoomApi.zoomedBarX(bar, zoom, basePx);
}

